# Nexly — AI Session Rules

## Tech Stack
- Next.js 15 (App Router) — no Pages Router
- TypeScript strict mode — no `any`
- Prisma ORM + PostgreSQL
- Better Auth for authentication
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components
- pnpm — never npm or yarn

## Design System
- Dark-themed UI, minimalist aesthetic
- Purple/lavender accents (`purple-500`, `violet-400`, etc.) for primary actions, CTAs, and highlights
- Consistent use of shadcn/ui primitives — do not reinvent common UI patterns
- Mobile-first responsive design on all new widgets and pages

## Architecture
- All API logic lives in `/app/api` or server actions — never in client components
- Prefer server components by default; use `"use client"` only when interactivity requires it
- Server actions are the preferred pattern for database mutations
- Always run `prisma migrate dev` after schema changes; never edit the DB directly

## Database
- Always read `prisma/schema.prisma` before suggesting UI changes that touch data
- Use proper Prisma migrations — never raw SQL patches outside of migration files
- Prefer typed Prisma queries; avoid casting results

## Coding Standards
- Clean, modular components — one responsibility per file
- No inline styles; use Tailwind utility classes
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks (`/hooks`) or utility files (`/lib`)
- No comments that narrate what the code does — only explain non-obvious intent

## State & Reactivity
- Optimistic updates where UX benefits; avoid unnecessary full-page reloads
- Prefer `useTransition` / `startTransition` for non-urgent state changes
- Server state (DB data) should be fetched server-side and passed as props

## Collaboration Features
- Use bidirectional approval logic for any brand ↔ creator collaboration flow
- Both parties must explicitly confirm before a collaboration is marked active

## Communication Style
- Be concise and technical
- Prioritize security and performance in all suggestions
- Surface trade-offs when multiple valid approaches exist
